The narrow-viewport layout - everything gated on viewport geometry (the
narrowGate overlay), independent of touch: a phone-width window shows one
workbench part fullscreen at a time, and one editor group.

- layout.ts: NARROW_PARTS fullscreen switching; boot pass seeds intent from
  the restored layout (parts restored visible fire no visibility event, so
  the first narrow pass would otherwise close what the user left open);
  rotation preserves the selected part; narrow close-part events from
  shell.js dismiss the fullscreen part on back gestures.
- Editor groups: editorPart.ts refuses to add one while the viewport is
  narrow, so everything that would split - the split commands, a SIDE_GROUP
  open, a tab dropped on an edge, an extension asking for a view column -
  opens as another tab in the group it was asked to split. A group that
  arrives without going through addGroup (a grid restored from a session on a
  wide screen, a copied group, a window dragged narrower) is merged into the
  active one on the next layout. editorGroupColumn.ts stops walking columns
  into existence once one does not appear rather than dereferencing the group
  it never got, falling back to SIDE_GROUP as it already does for a column
  out of range.
- Activity bar: `composery.activityBar.narrowLocation` (default `bottom`) is
  the narrow viewport's own activity bar location, independent of
  `workbench.activityBar.location` - one phone and one desktop browser share one
  Composery's settings file, so a single key would have had them moving each other's
  activity bar. narrowActivityBar.ts mirrors the narrow value onto the upstream
  key in the memory target so every existing reader and change listener needs no
  teaching, and configurationService.ts redirects writes of the upstream key to
  the narrow one while narrow, so repositioning from the phone stays there.
  workbench.ts installs the mirror between initServices and initLayout: any later
  and the first mirror fires a layout listener that dereferences a grid
  createWorkbenchLayout has not built yet.
  sidebarPart.ts keys its remembered last-visible-position storage off the same
  question, so hiding and unhiding the activity bar on a phone cannot restore the
  wide position into the narrow setting.
- Titlebar: the left flex region shrinks below the menubar's intrinsic width
  and the menubar flexes from zero, so MenuBar measures its real allocation
  and moves labels into overflow instead of pushing the title off-screen.
- Extension editor: feature tables get a picker (styled by narrow.css) and
  horizontal scrolling, so wide columns stay reachable.
- Webview content: narrow-width rules in the default stylesheet the webview pre
  page injects into extension content. Frame-independent on purpose - no
  viewport meta and no safe-area padding, because Chromium honours neither in a
  nested browsing context (measured on Android Chrome 134: a subframe declaring
  `width=500, viewport-fit=cover` laid out at the parent's 411px and read
  env(safe-area-inset-*) as 0 while the top frame read a real 24px bottom
  inset). What is left is border-box + max-width, so the 20px body padding
  cannot overflow a narrow viewport, plus the text-size rules. These styles live
  in a template literal inside the page's inline script, so the CSP hash beside
  them covers them; the patch-stack test recomputes it.

